WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.463
- Good morning believe nation!

00:00:01.463 --> 00:00:02.702
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.751
My one word is believe and I
believe that entrepreneurs will

00:00:05.751 --> 00:00:08.233
solve all of the world's major problems.

00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.531
So, to help you on your
journey today's message is

00:00:10.531 --> 00:00:12.781
ask great questions.

00:00:12.781 --> 00:00:14.511
Over to you, Timothy Ferris.

00:00:14.511 --> 00:00:16.995
(rooster crows)

00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:19.325
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:22.106
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:22.106 --> 00:00:25.606
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:31.082
- I find that levels of
success in almost any industry

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:35.330
or area correlate to a person
asking great questions.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.000
In some cases they seem absurd.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.830
In fact, the hallmark in a
way of great questions is they

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.740
sound completely ridiculous.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.389
So, someone might ask like
Peter Teal why can't you achieve

00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:47.771
your ten year goals in
the next six months?

00:00:47.771 --> 00:00:50.131
As a thought exercise
that's a good question to

00:00:50.131 --> 00:00:51.991
answer for yourself.

00:00:51.991 --> 00:00:56.719
And, these types of questions
come up surprisingly often

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:00.019
with very, very impressive folks.

00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:01.470
Whether it's in business, military,

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.930
entertainment or otherwise.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.590
And the way you can get
better at questions is by

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.901
studying interviews in part.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.688
So, I studied Larry King.

00:01:10.688 --> 00:01:12.058
I studied Charlie Rose.

00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:13.429
I studied Terry Gross.

00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:16.401
I studied dot, dot, dot,
dot go down the list.

00:01:16.401 --> 00:01:20.667
I studied Tony Robbins who
does in person one on one

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:23.029
interventions, at his events, in front of

00:01:23.029 --> 00:01:25.290
10,000, 5,000 people

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.509
and he's just genius with
how he uses his questions

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:31.789
to pattern interrupt and
grab someone's attention.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:34.999
And divert them in a more
productive direction.

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:36.069
Now, why is that relevant?

00:01:36.069 --> 00:01:39.661
It's relevant because
thinking is the process of

00:01:39.661 --> 00:01:41.138
asking questions.

00:01:41.138 --> 00:01:42.508
You're asking yourself questions and then

00:01:42.508 --> 00:01:43.890
you're answering them in your own head.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.389
So, if you get better at
asking other people questions

00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:48.458
you get better at asking
yourself questions.

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:52.490
Which means you are a,
you're improving your thought

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.831
performance and level of thinking.

00:01:55.831 --> 00:01:58.347
- One of my favorite exercises
to do both for myself and for

00:01:58.347 --> 00:02:01.540
entrepreneurs I'm working
with is try to turn limiting

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.040
statements into how questions.

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:07.679
Turn limiting statements
into how questions.

00:02:07.679 --> 00:02:11.598
So, as an example turn a
statement like "I can't do this

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.671
"because I have a full time job."

00:02:13.671 --> 00:02:17.309
into a, "How do I do this
while I have a full time job?"

00:02:17.309 --> 00:02:20.491
"I can't start this company
because I don't have any money."

00:02:20.491 --> 00:02:22.748
into, "How do I start this business

00:02:22.748 --> 00:02:24.434
"without having any money?"

00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:27.786
As soon as you can move
from a limiting statement

00:02:27.786 --> 00:02:29.145
that you absorbed and feel like,

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:29.978
"Well this is just true.

00:02:29.978 --> 00:02:31.824
"I have a limited life,
I have limited resources,

00:02:31.824 --> 00:02:33.295
"I have limited access, I'm limited,

00:02:33.295 --> 00:02:35.384
"everything's limited, I'm never
going to achieve anything."

00:02:35.384 --> 00:02:38.362
As soon as you can move
to that state to a how.

00:02:38.362 --> 00:02:42.425
How do I accomplish it with
these restrictions limitations?

00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:44.892
Then, you start to break out of your box.

00:02:44.892 --> 00:02:47.142
Then, you start to have the ability to do

00:02:47.142 --> 00:02:48.042
more amazing things.

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:51.080
And so it's catching yourself
to know when you're saying

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.323
really limiting statements
and then asking that question

00:02:54.323 --> 00:02:58.621
of how can I accomplish it
except these limitations

00:02:58.621 --> 00:02:59.716
that I have right now?

00:02:59.716 --> 00:03:01.957
Part of the challenge though,
is catching yourself and

00:03:01.957 --> 00:03:04.398
knowing when you're actually
saying a limiting statement.

00:03:04.398 --> 00:03:06.218
Because again, so much
of it is subconscious.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.586
So much of the time we're
just automatically living on

00:03:08.586 --> 00:03:09.419
small mode.

00:03:09.419 --> 00:03:11.268
Automatically living on auto-pilot.

00:03:11.268 --> 00:03:14.235
You know, living by other
peoples' expectations

00:03:14.235 --> 00:03:16.016
and limitations, constantly.

00:03:16.016 --> 00:03:19.047
We're not constantly going off
living big, doing huge things

00:03:19.047 --> 00:03:21.467
We're living in survival mode, so often.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.676
So, being able to catch
those limiting statements

00:03:23.676 --> 00:03:24.509
can be difficult.

00:03:24.509 --> 00:03:27.287
Here's one of my favorite
exercises on how to fix it.

00:03:27.287 --> 00:03:29.548
Look at yourself in the mirror.

00:03:29.548 --> 00:03:30.477
Go do it.

00:03:30.477 --> 00:03:31.609
Look yourself in the mirror.

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:33.368
Pull out your phone and look
at yourself in the phone.

00:03:33.368 --> 00:03:36.668
And ask yourself, "Why
am I not more successful?

00:03:36.668 --> 00:03:38.238
"Why have I not hit my goals?

00:03:38.238 --> 00:03:40.504
"Why have I had this goal
for the past three years,

00:03:40.504 --> 00:03:42.086
"and I haven't made progress on it?

00:03:42.086 --> 00:03:43.821
"Why is still a someday going?

00:03:43.821 --> 00:03:46.523
"Why have I not done a thing
that I said I want to do?

00:03:46.523 --> 00:03:48.654
"That seems important to
me, why have I not done it?"

00:03:48.654 --> 00:03:51.643
And somewhere in there is
going to be an answer, right?

00:03:51.643 --> 00:03:55.432
Like don't look away
until you get an answer.

00:03:55.432 --> 00:03:58.816
And then from there that's one
of your limiting statements.

00:03:58.816 --> 00:04:00.235
That's probably the most
powerful limiting statements

00:04:00.235 --> 00:04:01.965
that you have.

00:04:01.965 --> 00:04:04.432
Well, I'm not able to be a success.

00:04:04.432 --> 00:04:08.102
I haven't hit my goals because
I didn't go to University,

00:04:08.102 --> 00:04:11.983
and everybody who has
success went to University.

00:04:11.983 --> 00:04:14.144
That's the story you're having
to play over and over and

00:04:14.144 --> 00:04:15.926
over and over again, in your mind.

00:04:15.926 --> 00:04:17.255
Great, that's your limiting statement.

00:04:17.255 --> 00:04:19.316
Let's turn that into a how.

00:04:19.316 --> 00:04:21.115
How do I accomplish my
goal and start my business

00:04:21.115 --> 00:04:23.013
without having a University education?

00:04:23.013 --> 00:04:26.534
How do I surround myself
with resources and people

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:29.765
who prove to me that it's
possible to go off and do this?

00:04:29.765 --> 00:04:32.255
Because lots of entrepreneurs
have done it, right?

00:04:32.255 --> 00:04:34.172
So, I find that exercise so helpful.

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:35.626
Look at yourself in the mirror,

00:04:35.626 --> 00:04:38.057
or write down on a piece of paper.

00:04:38.057 --> 00:04:42.019
"I have not accomplished
my big goals yet because."

00:04:42.019 --> 00:04:43.537
Write it down, maybe
there's multiple reasons.

00:04:43.537 --> 00:04:44.759
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:47.209
Maybe fill up the whole
page of reasons why

00:04:47.209 --> 00:04:48.309
you're not more successful.

00:04:48.309 --> 00:04:49.789
Why you haven't hit your goals.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.599
Each one of those are limiting
statements and as long as you

00:04:52.599 --> 00:04:54.378
continue to believe them.

00:04:54.378 --> 00:04:56.665
As long as you continue to act by them,

00:04:56.665 --> 00:04:57.977
most of the time subconsciously.

00:04:57.977 --> 00:05:00.035
You sound just like you
everyday wake up and just remind

00:05:00.035 --> 00:05:02.846
yourself, I don't have
a university education

00:05:02.846 --> 00:05:04.148
so I'm not going to be successful.

00:05:04.148 --> 00:05:05.647
It's not like you have
these negative mantras,

00:05:05.647 --> 00:05:06.917
you just do it automatically.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.297
It's how you're living
your life by default.

00:05:09.297 --> 00:05:12.865
The limitations are hard to
catch because it's just default.

00:05:12.865 --> 00:05:16.158
So, if you build your list
of the reasons, and then

00:05:16.158 --> 00:05:17.308
start attacking them.

00:05:17.308 --> 00:05:18.807
Ask yourself how questions.

00:05:18.807 --> 00:05:21.728
As soon as you ask yourself how.

00:05:21.728 --> 00:05:23.368
How can I?

00:05:23.368 --> 00:05:26.349
Instead of just accepting
this negative situation,

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:28.538
the box starts to open.

00:05:28.538 --> 00:05:30.595
The possibilities start to open.

00:05:30.595 --> 00:05:33.457
You move from a negative
place to a positive one.

00:05:33.457 --> 00:05:37.849
You move from a resourceless
state to a resourceful one.

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:40.827
Because you're forcing your
brain to answer the question.

00:05:40.827 --> 00:05:45.140
How can I do this despite not
having this, or despite being

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.440
this or despite growing up like this?

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.099
How can I still go off and do it?

00:05:51.099 --> 00:05:53.545
And if you get used to
asking those potential filled

00:05:53.545 --> 00:05:56.169
questions then your brain
will find the answer.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:05:59.027
You're asking better
questions as opposed to living

00:05:59.027 --> 00:06:00.337
a really limited life.

00:06:00.337 --> 00:06:02.718
That for the most part is self-imposed.

00:06:02.718 --> 00:06:04.990
So the question of the day
today is, I'd like to quickly go

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.900
through this exercise with you.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.358
Ask yourself what is the one
reason, just pick one to start,

00:06:11.358 --> 00:06:13.225
you may have a huge list,
but what is one reason

00:06:13.225 --> 00:06:14.768
why you're not more successful?

00:06:14.768 --> 00:06:16.476
Why you haven't accomplished
your main goals?

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:18.427
Write it down on the comments
below and the next to it

00:06:18.427 --> 00:06:21.109
write down the how-to version of it.

00:06:21.109 --> 00:06:23.309
How can I, version of it.

00:06:23.309 --> 00:06:25.153
And if you really want to
take it to the next step,

00:06:25.153 --> 00:06:26.304
answer that question.

00:06:26.304 --> 00:06:27.959
Answer the how to.

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:31.247
I think if you do that
consistently, it'll open up a world

00:06:31.247 --> 00:06:33.787
of difference for you
and I'd love it to spark,

00:06:33.787 --> 00:06:35.307
right here on this episode.

00:06:35.307 --> 00:06:36.686
So, leave it down on the comments below.

00:06:36.686 --> 00:06:38.498
I'm really curios to see
what you have to say.

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:40.645
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Mike Philips.

00:06:40.645 --> 00:06:43.217
Mike thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:06:43.217 --> 00:06:44.276
Your One Word.

00:06:44.276 --> 00:06:46.034
I really appreciate the support Mike

00:06:46.034 --> 00:06:47.775
and I hope you enjoyed the book.

00:06:47.775 --> 00:06:49.679
So, thank you guys so much for watching.

00:06:49.679 --> 00:06:50.582
I believe in you.

00:06:50.582 --> 00:06:52.460
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.541
and what ever your one word is.

00:06:54.541 --> 00:06:56.140
Much love and I'll see you again

00:06:56.140 --> 00:07:00.000
tomorrow morning for
another shot of espresso.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.701
♫ I wake up every morning

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:06.201
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.847
- How would you tell us to
develop a habit of curiosity?

00:07:14.912 --> 00:07:16.482
- Well you know what, people do look at me

00:07:16.482 --> 00:07:18.849
and see me as creative because
I've written so many books.

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:23.528
And I am curious but when
I started off in college,

00:07:23.528 --> 00:07:27.256
in my freshman psych
class they gave us a test

00:07:27.256 --> 00:07:30.425
for each student to take on creativity.

00:07:30.425 --> 00:07:33.034
And there were 17 in that class,
I can remember it well, and

00:07:33.034 --> 00:07:36.200
I was at the bottom of
the class on creativity.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.254
I mean, I was like, number
17 out of 17 and the teacher

00:07:40.254 --> 00:07:43.639
posted the scores and
so I saw how bad I was.

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:45.980
And, then I really felt
bad not only because

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.761
I wasn't creative but I
was going to go pastor,

00:07:48.761 --> 00:07:50.206
I was going to go into ministry,
I was going to go teach,

00:07:50.206 --> 00:07:51.630
I was going to do sermons.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.994
And then I got to thinking to myself,

00:07:53.994 --> 00:07:57.564
"I'm going to be another boring preacher."

00:07:57.564 --> 00:07:59.043
And then that just gripped me I thought,

00:07:59.043 --> 00:07:59.876
"Oh, this is going to be awful.

00:07:59.876 --> 00:08:01.383
"I'm going to bore people all my life."

00:08:01.383 --> 00:08:02.216
You know what I mean?

00:08:02.216 --> 00:08:03.792
And then I'm going to ask them
to pay while they hear me.

00:08:03.792 --> 00:08:05.014
I mean, this is an awful deal.

00:08:05.014 --> 00:08:09.472
And so, two things I did, and
I talk about it in the book.

00:08:09.472 --> 00:08:10.985
One of them is I started filing.

00:08:10.985 --> 00:08:13.214
I started taking thoughts
and quotes, and the reason I

00:08:13.214 --> 00:08:15.231
started doing that is I
thought, "If I'm not going to be

00:08:15.231 --> 00:08:18.723
good at what I say, I need to
quote somebody that is good."

00:08:18.723 --> 00:08:21.335
And so I started filing things
that other people would say

00:08:21.335 --> 00:08:24.602
so I'd go around well Kim
said, you know what I mean and

00:08:24.602 --> 00:08:26.753
Joe said and Suzy said
and so that at least

00:08:26.753 --> 00:08:28.213
I would be a little more interesting

00:08:28.213 --> 00:08:30.634
and that's how it started.

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:33.722
But the big breakthrough, the
big breakthrough I had was,

00:08:33.722 --> 00:08:35.289
and this will help everyone if ...

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:36.752
How many of you would
like to be more creative?

00:08:36.752 --> 00:08:38.142
Let me see your hands, okay.

00:08:38.142 --> 00:08:41.683
How many of you'd like to
just absolutely have an

00:08:41.683 --> 00:08:43.622
imagination that just
took you to a new level?

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:44.455
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:44.455 --> 00:08:47.204
How many already have that
because you're on drugs?

00:08:47.204 --> 00:08:48.693
Okay, okay.

00:08:48.693 --> 00:08:51.823
Here's the key, this'll work for ya.

00:08:51.823 --> 00:08:55.573
I promise it'll work for you
because it worked for me.

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:58.493
I made a major change in my
early 30s from just having

00:08:58.493 --> 00:09:02.044
answers and being a leader
that just had to cast visions,

00:09:02.044 --> 00:09:04.912
show everybody what I was
doing and where we were going.

00:09:04.912 --> 00:09:08.182
And I started asking
intentional questions.

00:09:08.182 --> 00:09:11.312
And I became a person, I
ask questions all the time.

00:09:11.312 --> 00:09:13.405
Everyday I ask questions.

00:09:13.405 --> 00:09:17.964
And I have found just asking
questions will take you

00:09:17.964 --> 00:09:20.504
on exciting journeys
more than anything else.

00:09:20.504 --> 00:09:21.903
You're great, you excel in that Ken.

00:09:21.903 --> 00:09:22.761
I've watched you over the years.

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:23.923
You'd excel really in that.

00:09:23.923 --> 00:09:26.972
But for my early years I didn't
ask enough questions and so

00:09:26.972 --> 00:09:28.533
therefore I didn't get enough

00:09:28.533 --> 00:09:29.963
ideas and thoughts from others.

00:09:29.963 --> 00:09:31.592
So, when people say, "I
want to be more creative

00:09:31.592 --> 00:09:32.425
"What's step one?"

00:09:32.425 --> 00:09:34.193
Step number one is ask more questions.

00:09:34.193 --> 00:09:36.143
Just go to people and
start asking questions.

00:09:36.143 --> 00:09:38.053
It's amazing what you'll learn.

00:09:38.053 --> 00:09:41.472
- But I'm obsessed with the
idea of no standard terms.

00:09:41.472 --> 00:09:43.759
So, when we were first getting started

00:09:43.759 --> 00:09:47.622
and took financing, our
lawyers would take us

00:09:47.622 --> 00:09:49.111
through the documents and they'd say,

00:09:49.111 --> 00:09:51.401
"Oh, don't worry about
it, it's all standard."

00:09:51.401 --> 00:09:53.372
And at the time I'm
like, "Oh, sure standard.

00:09:53.372 --> 00:09:56.449
"That's great, no issues, that's standard.

00:09:56.449 --> 00:09:57.851
"No problem."

00:09:57.851 --> 00:10:00.321
And I've since learned that
standard means either the person

00:10:00.321 --> 00:10:04.309
who's walking through the
documents doesn't understand them.

00:10:04.309 --> 00:10:07.589
Or, you could be getting
taken advantage of.

00:10:07.589 --> 00:10:11.571
So, when someone say something
is standard just ask why.

00:10:11.571 --> 00:10:13.750
And why and why and why
until you really understand

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:16.586
intricately, I think, how
the deal is structured.

00:10:16.586 --> 00:10:19.799
I think, the big thing that
we took away from that is,

00:10:19.799 --> 00:10:22.787
you know, standard terms
is a huge red flag.

00:10:22.787 --> 00:10:24.670
And beyond that, when you're afraid,

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:25.727
just to continue asking why.

